index 23e9a31..cac9808 100644
--- a/diagnostics/README.md
+++ b/diagnostics/README.md
@@ -182,3 +182,97 @@ Drain Life now draws a green channel with animated strands and life motes moving
 from victim to caster. It reads authoritative active cast and matching aura source
 IDs, uses displayed unit positions, and stops on observed interruption, expiration
 or death. It adds no simulation, readback or dispatch work. Host lifecycle tests pass.
+
+
+## Warrior stances, Charge and Intercept (2026-10-30)
+
+The plan remains one u32 per unit/decision: spell byte 0, range byte 1,
+target byte 2, stance request bits 24..25. A zero stance request means keep the
+current stance. Switching never happens implicitly to satisfy an ability.
+Battle/Defensive/Berserker are explicit configuration/table state; the shared
+GPU engine applies a 1s stance lockout independent of spell GCD. Future plan genes
+may request abilities unavailable in the snapshot stance, so the engine can test
+stance transitions followed by abilities. First-step mutation checks resource
+availability after the requested legal stance change, including Rage retention.
+
+Default Forever assumptions: trainer Tactical Mastery retains 10 Rage, with no
+Improved Tactical Mastery assumed. `stanceRageRetention` is per-entity data (25
+models the additional 15 retention from five points). Defensive multiplies outgoing
+and incoming damage by 0.9. Berserker increases incoming damage by 10% and melee
+crit chance by 3 percentage points. Melee critical hits now use the entity's existing
+crit stat plus stance bonus, with deterministic event-keyed GPU RNG independent of
+mutation seeds/readback/commit size; the previous engine stored crit without using
+it. Threat has no outcome in this PvP engine and is not scored.
+
+Charge uses Battle, out-of-combat gating, 8–25 yd range, 15s cooldown, 15 Rage
+generation and a 1s stun. Intercept uses Berserker, 10 Rage, 8–25 yd range,
+30s cooldown, 100 base physical damage and a 3s stun. CC applies at activation;
+movement travels at the simulator's explicit 42 yd/s to 3 yd from the target's
+activation position. One arrival event terminates each dash; positions are evaluated
+from absolute start/end times so arbitrary commit partitions agree. Collision uses
+a swept segment against inflated pillars and bounds. Roots and incapacitation
+cancel an active dash at its authoritative current position. Charge/Intercept do
+not use normal walking to secretly satisfy the minimum-range check.
+
+Combat state is refreshed by hostile resolutions, damage ticks and assistance to
+an ally already in combat, with a 5s inactivity timeout. Target selection/movement
+alone never enters combat. Controlled stun DR is separate from Fear DR. The engine
+retains a deterministic 15s reset; exact Forever PvP DR group/reset fidelity and
+charge transit speed need in-game validation. Existing Shield Wall numerical tuning,
+shield equipment requirements, hit tables, armor formula, Rage generation and other
+legacy mechanics are not made fully Classic-accurate by this expansion.
+
+Rules references: [Classic stance implementation](https://github.com/wowsims/classic/blob/master/sim/warrior/stances.go),
+[Forever client stance/macro data](https://foreverdb.net/class/Warrior/macros),
+[Forever Berserker tooltip](https://foreverdb.net/spell/2458).
+The Forever-specific baseline retention follows extracted client data; talent choices
+are configuration, never planner behavior rules.
+
+`npm run test:gpu-native` compiles the production WGSL plus the diagnostic prediction
+entry point with Naga, builds `tests/native-gpu.c`, and executes compute through Vulkan.
+It needs a C compiler, Vulkan headers/loader, an ICD, and `naga` (`NAGA` overrides its
+path). It defaults to Lavapipe when available; `VK_DRIVER_FILES` selects a different
+ICD. Fixtures execute production live/prediction/mutation entry points, with no CPU
+combat reference. It checks legality, retention, cooldowns, GCD-independent switching,
+collision, roots, transit across commits, stance modifiers and opposing-population
+isolation. It also exercises a short root that expires before the next dash event.
+Browser validation remains deferred; successful software Vulkan execution establishes
+native compute behavior, not browser compiler compatibility or hardware throughput.
+
+Performance costs to flag: EntityState grew from 100 to 115 words (+15%), and ArenaState
+from 2480 to 2840 bytes (+14.5%). The private arena+queue footprint grew from 3448 to
+3808 bytes (+10.4%) before compiler optimization/register allocation. Each dash adds
+one queue event; active dashes trigger bounded position/cancellation checks. Extra
+stance decisions during an existing spell GCD occur only when the next gene requests
+a supported different stance. A 64-byte stance uniform was added; no new readback or
+compute pass was added to production. Plan buffers, workgroup size, horizon and
+population limits are unchanged. Generic movement and explicit stance search enlarge
+the search space, so fixed-time planning quality needs measurement too.
+
+There is no physical GPU exposed in this sandbox. **No hardware throughput regression
+has been established or ruled out.** Compare fixed-roster, fixed-seed benchmark runs
+at the same population/time budget using `runBatchBenchmark`, and compare live queue
+latency/plan age using exported scheduling data. The 15% entity growth is a known
+memory cost, not a prediction of a 15% slowdown.
+
+## Failed gap-closer movement correction (2026-10-30)
+
+Charge/Intercept eligibility is now evaluated at the start of the decision, after
+the explicit stance request and resource update. A legal dash replaces walking;
+a rejected dash preserves the plan's independent desired-range movement, including
+retreat or holding position. A rejected dash cannot become a cast later in that
+same decision because walking crossed its range boundary. Range, stance, combat,
+resource, GCD, cooldown, root, LoS and collision requirements still apply.
+
+Rendering distinguishes an authoritative active dash from a selected spell and
+continues to extrapolate the planned range movement after rejected attempts. It
+does not duplicate ability legality/combat simulation in TypeScript.
+
+Added 11 native GPU scenarios, bringing the suite to 39 live/prediction scenarios
+plus mutation (79 executions). The additions check failed approach/retreat,
+wrong stance, combat status, cooldown, GCD, resource shortage, collision, range
+boundaries, a legal near-minimum-range dash, roots and ordinary caster movement.
+Host checks and the build also pass. Search scheduling, population, mutation,
+state/buffer sizes, dispatches and readbacks are unchanged. Hardware timing and
+browser verification remain unmeasured/deferred. This fixes failed-dash freezes;
+it does not resolve stance churn or the adaptive throughput estimator issue.
